#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Generate a HUD surface for a request nobody built a surface for.

Fixed surfaces (the week, what is overdue, who you owe a message) cover the
questions somebody thought of in advance. Everything else used to get a
sentence on the pill. This asks a model for a layout instead, and it keeps the
model to the job a model is good at and away from the two it is bad at:

- The model picks components from a closed catalog (config/genui/catalog.json, checked
  against hud/CLAUDE.md and the Swift renderer) and writes Kyber Lines.
- The model never sees the data. It binds props to pointers like
  `@/q/due:14/events`, and the resolver here runs the real query and sends the
  `d` line. A number on the glass is a number something read.
- The model never invents an action. A Button's action must be in
  config/genui/policy.json, and anything else is refused before it is drawn.

Every line is validated before it reaches the socket, the whole surface is
validated at the end, one repair attempt gets the precise errors, and after
that the request falls back to a plain status panel. The skeleton (Screen and
`r`) is on the glass before the model has been asked anything.

Usage:
    kyber-genui "<request>" [--dry-run] [--pin NAME] [--at REGION] [--json]
    kyber-genui preset NAME [--dry-run]       redraw a pinned surface, fresh data, no model
    kyber-genui presets                       list pinned surfaces
    kyber-genui fixed NAME [--at REGION]      draw an exemplar's fixed layout with fresh data
    kyber-genui validate [FILE|-]             validate Kyber Lines as a generated surface
    kyber-genui catalog [--check|--prompt]    the vocabulary, or the drift check
    kyber-genui queries                       the query vocabulary the model may bind to
    kyber-genui event <action> <component>    handle a press on a generated surface
    kyber-genui bench [--requests FILE] [--out FILE] [--only N,..] [--regrade RESULTS]
    kyber-genui keep [NAME]                   the last model-written surface becomes a framework candidate
    kyber-genui candidates                    candidates held back, and the promoted ones
    kyber-genui promote NAME                  check a candidate against the sealed requests, then use it

Before the model there are two tiers (bin/lib/genui_tiers.py): Tier 0 redraws
an exemplar, preset or promoted framework the request matches near-exactly,
and Tier 1 has Jev pick an archetype and a source, then lays the panel out
from the shape of the data that came back. GENUI_TIERS=0,1,2 picks which run.

Design and sources: docs/reference/GENUI.md.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

